    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (BUILD SUCCESS). \n\nThe change is complete. Here's what I implemented for the \"reject when 100+ owners registered today \u2192 429\" rule, following the existing per-city-capacity pattern:\n\n1. **`OwnerDailyRegistrationLimitExceededException`** (new, in `rest/controller/`) \u2014 a domain exception signalling the daily cap was hit, mirroring `OwnerCityCapacityExceededException`.\n\n2. **`ClinicService.countOwnersRegisteredOn(LocalDate)`** \u2014 new interface method, implemented in `ClinicServiceImpl` alongside `countOwnersInCity`/`countNamesakes`, counting owners whose `registrationDate` matches the given date.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a `MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100` constant and a guard that throws the new exception when `countOwnersRegisteredOn(owner.getRegistrationDate()) >= 100`. The check runs after the registration date is defaulted to today, so the count is against the correct day.\n\n4. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 a handler mapping the new exception to **HTTP 429 (Too Many Requests)**, consistent with the other owner-rule handlers.\n\nEach piece sits in its established home (validation/count logic in the service, HTTP mapping in the advice, orchestration in the controller), reusing the existing structure rather than duplicating it.",
